window.ATLAS = {"lands": {"Iuga Domini": {"names": [["Church Latin", "the Ridges of the Lord"]], "lore": [["The Holy Domain", "The oldest Church land, and the one that looks most like the Church wants the world to look. The northern ridges carry the City of Lanterns in the husk of the first Cathedral; the middle country is fields, tithe-barns and parish churches with bells big enough to frighten a demon; the south goes to forest where the Sylvan Veil tribes keep their Sacred Trees and their distance. Its east coast faces the Eye across Mare Stellarum, and Echo Bay was the first port in the world to send a ship out."], ["How places are named", "Anything the Church built carries a Church Latin name, and the faithful translate it into plain pious English for daily use. That's why the same city is the City of Lanterns on a writ and Candlecross in a tavern. Village names run older and plainer (Kartoffel, Dunridge, Titheford) and only take on a saint when a priest has had a hand in them. The old tongue survives only underground, in Talarith mouths, and in a few Sylvan Veil words the Church never bothered to correct."], ["Who holds it", "In practice, the Church. Theurgists outrank any local authority, Oculates keep the city watches, and Funerists sign the dead into the parish registers. The old noble houses still own land and titles, but a lord who quarrels with a Theurgist rarely keeps either."]]}, "Pratum Putritum": {"names": [["Church Latin", "the Rotting Meadow"]], "lore": [["The Rotting Meadow", "This was the holiest pilgrimage land there was, because Mount Terebra was thought to touch Caelum. The monks of Ur-Ignis climbed it, saw light leaking through a tear in the sky and came down rotting. Now it is the Heart of Godrot, a coast sealed by every branch of the Theurgists, with the Colonies festering at the mountain's foot. The Church still owns every place-name here, and none of them fit any more."], ["How places are named", "Pilgrim Latin, worn down. The pilgrims named everything for the climb (the Stair, the Hospice, the Wound), and the colonists kept the words and stripped the holiness off. The Scala is the Scale, the Cingulum is the Belt, and Saint Lazar is a verb: to be lazared is to be sent in."], ["Life inside", "Water and food are held by crime families, and information is worth more than either. Healthy flesh is currency in the literal sense. People say 'flesh keep me' and 'let me be remembered', and they mean both."]]}, "Lignum Stagnae": {"names": [["Church Latin", "the Timber of Still Waters"]], "lore": [["Tomb of the Colossus", "Under the 4th Archbeholder an eldritch colossus fell out of the sky into the sea, and its corpse became this land. The skin is iridescent scale under thin soil, the wings stone into the western cliffs, and the faceless head a basin of marsh. Sanguva vines grow from it as if it were feeding them. The Church named it 'timber' because the first landers took the ribs for drowned trees."], ["How places are named", "The first landers named things for what they took them to be, in the farmers' Latin of the time, and the names stuck after they understood. Vinea is the vineyard, Rubicun the reddening, Scelis the crime, and the Costa is just 'the rib'. Nobody in Lignum says 'colossus' in daily speech. They say 'him'."], ["Three peoples", "Vinea's harvesters in the east, Rubicun's marsh-folk on the head, and Scelis's smugglers under the wings. They trade with each other, distrust each other, and all hold the same rite on the same night for a spirit none of them will name."]]}, "Anima Bestia": {"names": [["Church Latin", "the Soul of the Beast"]], "lore": [["The land", "This is where the Frenatores bind their beasts. More animals are born here on a Closing night than anywhere else in Etelen, and some come back afterwards from Gehenna or Caelum on their own. It has few people, many herds and a kennel in every village. The Church holds the north coast and the fortress of the Domus Bestiarum and leaves the interior to herders it calls heathen and doesn't bother to convert."], ["The old tongue", "The Church came here late, so this is the one place where the old tongue is still spoken above ground, the same tongue the Talarith keep beneath the Ossuar Spine. Most places have two names, a Church Latin one on the writs and an old-tongue one the herders actually use. Scholars in the City argue over whose language it was before the Scourging, and nobody has asked the herders."], ["What the herders believe", "They don't worship the Eye or the Blasphemer. They count. Every herd is counted morning and night, and a beast that appears in the count unexplained is kept, named and never sold. Their word for the Closing is Vaelun, the unseen hour, and they treat it as a season rather than a sin."]]}, "The seas": {"names": [["Church Latin", "Mare Stellarum and Mare Scelus"]], "lore": [["Controlled water", "Sailing is controlled by the Church. Every voyage needs a Pelagic Writ, every licensed ship carries a Witness navigator, and private sailing is piracy. The Sea of Stars lies north of the Eye and the Sea of Crime to the south. Sailors split it more simply, into the water the Church can see and the water it can't."], ["What the Registry knows", "Annex VI (Pelagic) holds the Church's ocean lore: the Black Meridian, the Pale Expanse, the lost Saint Ordel, a warning that the stars become unreliable, an island marked with a closed eye, and logs of Temporal Discrepancies. None of it is explained."], ["What the sailors know", "Once a decade the shadows pass under Echo Bay. The Threnody Leviathan drags ships on chains. Smugglers tell of a flat calm with lights under the hull, after which a boy was simply gone from the deck, and the captain swore that what took him wasn't swimming."]]}}, "places": [{"id": "city-of-lanterns", "name": "City of Lanterns", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 1, "x": 17, "y": 17, "names": [["pious English", "Candlecross"], ["Church Latin", "Civitas Lucernarum"]], "lore": [["The husk", "The first Cathedral of the Eye was built in these mountains before the Church moved its heart out to sea. What was left behind was too large to pull down, so a city grew inside it. Streets run along the old nave aisles, houses are built into side-chapels, and the outermost wall was once a choir screen. The scale is wrong everywhere: doorways three storeys high, a market under a vault you can't see the top of. Folk say the hymns still echo in the stone, and at night the City does nothing to discourage the story."], ["The rings", "The City is built in rings. Outside the Walls is where pilgrims sleep, and the Ash Wards are where it puts whatever it burns. Then come the Lower Streets, the Pilgrim Roads and the Lantern Streets, where every door keeps a lamp lit from the Lantern. The Scholar's Quarter holds the Grand Athenaeum and, five levels beneath it, the Grand Lantern Registry and its twelve annexes. At the centre is the Sanctum Ignis, where the Lantern burns in its housing and the Lantern Keepers walk their chanting circle without stopping."], ["The Lantern", "The Lantern holds a flame that Oculti is said to have lit with his own gaze, and it is the City's reason for existing. While it burns on a Closing night, nobody inside the walls turns, and the City has never lost a citizen to the Accursed within them. The Ministri Ignis tend it. Their rules on who may approach it, when, and carrying what, fill a book thicker than the Optinomikon."], ["The calendar of light", "The Ceremony of Eternal Light falls on the night before each Closing. Theurgists come to be blessed, and every household relights its lamp from the eternal flame. The Festival of the Shining Path is a week of debate in which thousands of new lanterns are hung. The Lantern Vigil falls on the last night of every month and is kept in total silence. The First Witness is painted on nearly every wall: where the Eye looked, flesh woke, memory followed, and the Lantern was kindled."]], "rumour": "Umorum was born here. Under the 13th Archbeholder, workmen opening a cellar beneath the City found vials of old blood, and the first blood-spells were drawn from them. Nobody has found the rest of that cellar."}, {"id": "arx-amonis", "name": "Arx Amonis", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 13, "y": 13.5, "names": [["pious English", "Amon's Keep"]], "lore": [["The Venatores' school", "Amon was orphaned in the 257th Closing, and the keep stands on the site of the farm where it happened. It's where young Venatores learn Vigilus Oculus, the Awakened Eye. The training is famously simple. Novices sit in an unlit cell through one whole Closing with the door unbarred, and the ones who come out still able to see what isn't there are kept."], ["Brother Korval's rule", "The keep still runs on the rule of the Bellator who raised Amon. Every Venator owns one blade, one lamp and one name, and keeps nothing that would make a beast hesitate to kill them. They hunt alone, so the keep feels like a monastery whose monks never talk to each other."]], "rumour": "Every hundred years the keep's novices sit their Closing in the cell where Amon himself waited out the 257th. Some of them come out describing a man in the corner who never spoke."}, {"id": "domus-eliadae", "name": "Domus Eliadae", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 21, "y": 12.6, "names": [["pious English", "the She-Saint's House"]], "lore": [["Mind, flesh, soul", "This is the Sanatores' surgery-monastery, founded by Arioch in Eliadah's honour on the ridge above the City. Strayed are brought up the switchback road on stretchers to be purified or salvaged. The cloister garden grows the poppies and willow that the anaesthetics would be made from, if the Sanatores believed in anaesthetic. The Warsurgeons use them instead."], ["The relic", "Eliadah died destroying the Traitor of the Eye, an Archbeholder turned Accursed. Her surgical saw hangs over the altar, still dark at the teeth. The order's motto is carved under it, and novices are made to read it aloud before their first amputation: in the end, we all bleed the same."]], "rumour": "The Mutators' records describe every graft they've ever made, with drawings. A few pages are written in a hand nobody in the order recognises, describing grafts nobody in the order admits to."}, {"id": "cantoria-iabalis", "name": "Cantoria Iabalis", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "holy", "size": 3, "x": 19.6, "y": 20, "names": [["pious English", "Jabal's Loft"]], "lore": [["The choir house", "A long, narrow house on the City's outer slope where the Arias train. Jabal was born mute and sang the Rite of Light with Oculti's own voice, so the novices spend their first year forbidden to speak, singing only. One captain of the Seventh Choir has had his name chiselled off the captains' board, and the gap was never filled in."]]}, {"id": "ossuar-spine", "name": "The Ossuar Spine", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "wild", "size": 2, "x": 24, "y": 20, "names": [["Church Latin", "Spina Ossuaria"]], "lore": [["The ridge", "A long ridge of pale rock between the coast and the City, cut by one canyon pass. The Church currently holds the pass under blockade, so travellers who can't show the right papers either wait or go underneath it."], ["Why the name", "Spina Ossuaria means the Bone-House Spine. Shepherds will tell you the ridge was named for its colour. The Talarith underneath could tell you what it is actually lying on."]]}, {"id": "talarith", "name": "The Talarith Deeps", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 21.6, "y": 23.2, "names": [["old tongue", "Tal-Vorrath, 'the place beneath the great body'"]], "lore": [["The people below", "The Talarith live in the caves and old workings under the Spine and worship the buried titan Vorr-Khal. They are the only people in Iuga Domini who still speak the old tongue every day. Their strongest hold the office of Burden-Bearer, and they call anyone who uses magic a Picker, their word for a scavenger who strips a carcass."], ["The sacrifice", "The Talarith give their own to Vorr-Khal, lowering them into a shaft at the deepest point of the Deeps. Very rarely one survives and climbs back out. The survivor is outcast, called sael-, 'far from', and given the god's name after it, so that every exile carries their own disgrace in their name."], ["Trade with the surface", "There isn't any, officially. Unofficially the Talarith buy lamp-oil, iron and above all lanterns lit in Candlecross, which they value above silver. Nobody on the surface has ever been told what they do with them."]], "rumour": "A lantern from Candlecross buys passage through the Deeps. Promise one and fail to deliver, and the Talarith remember."}, {"id": "dunridge", "name": "Dunridge", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 26.2, "y": 26, "lore": [["Under the Spine", "A grey market town at the foot of the Ossuar Spine that lives off the canyon pass. When the Church closes the pass, which it does whenever something on the far side needs keeping quiet, Dunridge lives off the people waiting for it to reopen. That means inns charging triple, guides of mixed honesty and a chapel doing brisk business in travellers' blessings. Its people are famous for knowing exactly which questions not to ask an Oculate."]], "rumour": "Every guide in Dunridge claims to know the way under the Spine. Two of them actually do, and one of those two works for the Oculates at the pass."}, {"id": "ostium-gratiae", "name": "Ostium Gratiae", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 28.4, "y": 31, "names": [["locals", "the Ostium"]], "lore": [["Mouth of Grace", "The Church's receiving port for traffic from Pratum Putritum. Supply hulls come home empty and clerks come home with ledgers. On the mole is a quarantine house where anyone landing from a colony boat waits nine days to see whether their veins darken. Those who pass are blessed and released. Those who don't are put back on the boat. The town is clean, quiet and very well lit, and it smells of lye."]], "rumour": "The quarantine house keeps a book of every name that passed through. Fewer names went back on the boats than were ever recorded as arriving in the Colonies."}, {"id": "echo-bay", "name": "Echo Bay", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 1, "x": 27.6, "y": 14, "labelLeft": true, "names": [["Church Latin", "Sinus Echus"]], "lore": [["First to sail", "The northeastern port was the first in the world to put to sea and has never let anyone forget it. It's rich on trade and proud of a navy whose captains sit in the front pews. Every shipping house's door carries a carved ship and a Pelagic Writ number. The Church's naval offices here issue more writs than anywhere outside the Cathedral."], ["The breath", "About once a decade colossal shadows pass beneath the bay. The fish vanish, the water goes flat, and the town says the sea is holding its breath. Nothing has ever been attacked. Every old fisher has stood on the harbour wall and watched one pass, and most won't describe it."], ["The Echoing of the Tides", "At every full moon the whole town makes the sound of waves with shells, bells and reeds, until the fish come back. In spring the Festival of the Sea Winds launches the year's new hulls. Sailors' oaths here are 'Seven Hells' and 'Black water take it'."]], "rumour": "Any licensed ship bound for the open sea leaves from Echo Bay. Any unlicensed one goes out of its way to avoid it."}, {"id": "custos-velatus", "name": "Custos Velatus", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "monument", "size": 1, "x": 29.6, "y": 11.4, "names": [["pious English", "the Veiled Watcher"], ["sailors", "Old Blindman"]], "lore": [["The statue", "Robert, the First Archbeholder, stands three hundred feet tall at the mouth of Echo Bay, carved from a single sea-stack. He wears a bronze veil over his eyes, the Veil of Sight that every Archbeholder wears after blinding himself. His right arm holds a lantern out over the water. Every ship leaving port passes under a blind man's light, and that was the point: the first sailors were told they'd be seen even where they couldn't see."], ["The lantern", "The lantern is a room. Twelve keepers live in the statue's chest and climb his arm by an inner stair to feed the flame. It burns oil pressed in the City and is lit from the Lantern itself, carried down the coast road each year after the Ceremony of Eternal Light."], ["The plinth", "The plinth is carved with the names of every Echo Bay ship that never came back, with the oldest names at eye level and the newest near the waterline. The masons stopped leaving room years ago. New names now go on the rock of the sea-stack itself, below the tideline, and you can only read them at low water."], ["Old Blindman", "Sailors touch the plinth going out and spit going in. Inside the town nobody says Robert, only Old Blindman, and only fondly."]], "rumour": "When the shadows pass under the bay, the lantern's flame leans out to sea against the wind, and the keepers bar themselves inside until it straightens."}, {"id": "pons-peregrinorum", "name": "Pons Peregrinorum", "land": "The seas", "kind": "monument", "size": 1, "x": 38.2, "y": 42.6, "names": [["pious English", "the Pilgrims' Bridge"], ["sailors", "the Penitents' Teeth"]], "lore": [["The road to the Eye", "When the Cathedral moved to the heart of the ocean, an Archbeholder ordered a road built to it so that the faithful could walk to the Eye. Five centuries of Disgraced labour raised a line of arches from Iuga Domini's east shore across the shoals. Every stone is stamped with the oculi sigil of the penitent who set it, so each one is a name the Church struck from its books and then built on."], ["Where it stops", "It ends forty miles out at a half-arch with no keystone. The Archbeholder who ordered it died, the next one didn't renew the decree, and the Disgraced were sent elsewhere. Nobody has formally cancelled the work, so in the Church's ledgers the bridge is still being built."], ["The walk", "Pilgrims walk it anyway. It takes three days out, sleeping in the arch-cells the masons left, and ends at a small chapel on the last pier, where they wait for the boat the Church sends when it chooses. Some wait months. At low tide the shoals under the bridge are white with the bones of those who got tired of waiting and tried to swim."], ["The Teeth", "From the sea the arches look like a jaw, so sailors call it the Penitents' Teeth. They give it a wide berth in fog, because the currents between the piers are strong enough to pull a hull into the stone."]], "rumour": "On a dead-calm night you can see a second row of arches from the last pier, under the water, running the same way toward the Eye."}, {"id": "sylvan-veil", "name": "The Sylvan Veil", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "wild", "size": 2, "x": 22, "y": 42, "names": [["Church Latin", "Velum Silvae"]], "lore": [["The forest", "The southern forest is broadleaf near the fields and older and darker toward the coast. Semi-nomadic tribes live in it, and their homes leave no trace when they move. You can stand in a clearing where three hundred people slept last week and find only flattened grass."], ["Genetrix", "The tribes worship Genetrix, a formless mother-soul of nature dwelling in an outer realm and present in each tribe's Sacred Tree. Druids train by passing through the trees' roots. Offerings are made the morning after each Closing. The Blessing of the Bow and the Feast of the Green mark the year."], ["The Church", "The Veil is autonomous in name and defended in practice. Theurgists come in on Closing nights to hold the line against the Accursed and leave in the morning. The tribes tolerate it and don't thank them."]]}, {"id": "taldren", "name": "Taldren", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 25, "y": 47.4, "names": [["old tongue", "'the hand beneath'"], ["Church records", "Arbor Matris"]], "lore": [["The oldest Tree", "The oldest Sacred Tree in the Veil stands on the cliffs where the forest meets the sea. The tribes keep its old-tongue name, Taldren, the hand beneath, because its roots go down through the rock into the salt water like fingers reaching for something. Every Veil druid who trains through the roots eventually comes out here."], ["The Church's version", "Church records list it as Arbor Matris, the Mother's Tree, and describe it as a pagan shrine of no importance. Three separate Theurgist reports, filed a century apart, recommend felling it. All three were approved and none was carried out."]], "rumour": "The tree hums when something moves in the deep tunnels under the coast, and the druids have a word for the pressure it puts behind the ears. Some people born near the sea feel it without being druids at all."}, {"id": "kartoffel", "name": "Kartoffel", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 14, "y": 38, "lore": [["The village", "A farming village in the middle country, about four days on foot from the City and a day from the coast. It has fields, a small church, a blacksmith and a general store, and it has never been drawn on a map before. It is honestly ordinary. Its priest preaches that the sea is demonic, and the congregation, who have never seen it, agree."], ["No Lantern", "Kartoffel, like most villages in Iuga Domini, has no Lantern of its own. On Closing nights its people bar their doors, ring the church bell until their arms give out, and hope. The Church stocks a shelter on the City road for those who can reach it. Most don't try."]]}, {"id": "vesperwick", "name": "Vesperwick", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 3, "x": 17.6, "y": 40.2, "lore": [["The mill village", "Kartoffel's nearest neighbour, half a day east along the cart track, with a mill and two dozen houses. It turned worse than its neighbours on the last Closing, and the Church has whitewashed a closed eye on six of its front doors. Those houses are empty. Nobody on the street will say whether the families inside were killed or taken."]], "rumour": "One whitewashed door has been scrubbed clean again from the inside."}, {"id": "sextons-halt", "name": "Sexton's Halt", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 3, "x": 16.6, "y": 33.4, "lore": [["Funerists' waystation", "A walled yard on the City road where the Funerists rest their hearse-wagons. Every body going to the City for burial or study stops here a night and is checked for signs that it wants to get up again. The keeper is a cheerful old Funerist named Brother Odo who has seen more undead risings than anyone alive, and he doesn't believe in Once-Men 'turning back'."]], "rumour": "After each Closing the yard fills up, and some wagons arrive under the seal of the Office of Doctrinal Stability with their lids nailed shut. Brother Odo is not allowed to check those."}, {"id": "drowned-hart", "name": "The Drowned Hart", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 15.6, "y": 28.4, "lore": [["The shelter tavern", "A roadside tavern on the City road with a hart's head over the door, its antlers green with damp. Because it stands halfway between the villages and the City, the Church stocks it as a Closing shelter, with barred shutters, a deep cellar, bread, blessed water and a bell. Travellers caught on the road on Closing night are told to make for the Hart."]], "rumour": "The landlord counts the shelterers in and out on every Closing night. He has started finding that his numbers don't match."}, {"id": "cerafax", "name": "Cerafax", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 10.8, "y": 30.2, "names": [["pious English", "Waxbright"]], "lore": [["The wick-town", "Every lantern relit at the Ceremony of Eternal Light is wicked with Cerafax cord and fed with Cerafax tallow. The town is mostly rendering-houses, and the smell reaches the road a mile before the walls do. The charter over the chapel door names it a holy industry."], ["The tallow", "The fat comes from what the Church hauls off the field after each Skirmish. It's blessed first, and nobody asks. Accursed fat burns brighter and longer than mutton, which the masters will tell you is proof of something, though they'd rather not say what."]], "rumour": "After the last Closing the carts came in full, and some of the carcasses still had clothes on."}, {"id": "turibulum", "name": "Turibulum", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 9, "y": 22, "names": [["miners", "Tubb"]], "lore": [["Haskite town", "Turibulum means censer. It's a mining town in the western uplands, and most smoke-vessels outside the Church's own forges begin as its ore. Tarnished haskite comes out of these hills grey and soft enough to dent with a thumbnail, which is why the Alkemi Ordained keep the right to make pure haskite for themselves."], ["The miners", "Miners burn their own reagents at shift's end to clear their lungs, so the town sits under a permanent low haze that smells of juniper and pennyroyal. You can tell a Tubb man by the yellow under his nails and by how he never breathes in through his mouth."]], "rumour": "A new seam holds smoke without ever being boiled in accursed blood. The Alkemi Ordained want the shaft collapsed before word gets out."}, {"id": "hallowmere", "name": "Hallowmere", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 3, "x": 6.6, "y": 33, "lore": [["The drowned parish", "A village on a black lake near the western rim. A hundred years ago the lake rose overnight and took the church, and the bell can still be seen on clear days, standing upright on the bottom. The Church built a new church on the shore and forbade anyone to fish over the old one. The villagers swear the drowned bell rang once, underwater, on the last Closing."]]}, {"id": "titheford", "name": "Titheford", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 20.6, "y": 34, "lore": [["The alchemist's rumour", "A market town at a river crossing where the Church once collected its tithes in kind; the tithe-barn is the largest building in it. It's also the rumoured hideout of Nicholas Flamel, which the townsfolk deny with the bored speed of people who are asked about it every week."]], "rumour": "An alchemist's shop on the bridge has sold the same three remedies for two hundred years, all in the same unchanging hand. The townsfolk find this unremarkable."}, {"id": "the-cove", "name": "Cala Signi", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "wild", "size": 2, "x": 9.4, "y": 45.4, "names": [["pious English", "the Marked Cove"], ["smugglers", "Dead Men's Cove"]], "lore": [["The sign", "A narrow cove on the coast west of Kartoffel. Above the tideline, a symbol is cut deep into the rock: not a Church mark, not a ship's mark, something geometric that the eye slides off. Smugglers used the cove for generations because patrols wouldn't put in under it."], ["The dead", "Smuggling crews that ran out of this cove buried their own here, in the rock shelves above the sign, without priests or registers. The sailors call it Dead Men's Cove and say a crew that sails from here owes the cove one man."]]}, {"id": "cathedral", "name": "Cathedral of Oculti", "land": "The seas", "kind": "holy", "size": 1, "x": 50, "y": 50, "names": [["Church Latin", "Cathedralis Oculi"]], "lore": [["At the heart of the ocean", "A cathedral the size of a country stands beneath the Eye at the centre of the world. It is the Church's nerve centre and home to the Archbeholder and every member of the Sacred Triumvirate. Pilgrims who reach it describe a coastline of buttresses rather than a building, and they say the bells can be heard a day's sail out."], ["Who sits here", "The Archbeholder, ritually blinded and wearing the Veil of Sight, is believed to see the holy and the accursed across all of Etelen. The current one is Solon, the 67th. The Alkemi Sanctus keep their vaults here and alone know the Transfiguratio Sacra. The High Theurgists of the three Leagues answer here. The Office of Doctrinal Stability keeps its archive in the lower galleries."], ["What's beneath", "There is a library large enough that its assistants are issued maps. Somewhere under the floor lies the tome from which Enoch, the 57th Archbeholder, learned Dominus Bestia. It was sealed again after he slaughtered his way through the 345th Closing, and below it are vaults nobody outside the Triumvirate has seen."]]}, {"id": "portus-oculi", "name": "Portus Oculi", "land": "The seas", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 50, "y": 63.4, "names": [["sailors", "the Writ-House"]], "lore": [["Where writs are issued", "The Cathedral's sea-gate on its southern face is a walled harbour where every Pelagic Writ in Etelen is countersigned and every Witness navigator is sworn. Captains queue for weeks. The building has one window, high up, and the clerks call it the Eye's Eye. A writ without its wax seal from this harbour is worthless, and a forged seal carries a sentence to the Colonies."]], "rumour": "Every writ's bearing is copied into a second ledger that never leaves the building. Captains who sail somewhere unusual find an Examiner waiting when they come home."}, {"id": "black-meridian", "name": "The Black Meridian", "land": "The seas", "kind": "sea", "size": 2, "x": 50, "y": 22.5, "lore": [["The dotted line", "Registry charts give this name to the dotted line drawn north from the Eye on every chart. Witness navigators won't cross it after dark, and any ship that does has to log the crossing in its writ to the minute. The superstition is old enough that captains don't know where it came from, only that they won't be the first to ignore it."]]}, {"id": "unreliable-stars", "name": "Here the Stars Become Unreliable", "land": "The seas", "kind": "sea", "size": 2, "x": 58, "y": 9.5, "lore": [["The warning", "Old charts carry this phrase in capitals across the far north of Mare Stellarum. Beyond it the constellations drift and close into sigils, and navigators who steer by them reach places they never logged. Temporal Discrepancies in Registry ship logs cluster here: ships logged home a day before they left, or a week after their crews had aged a year."]]}, {"id": "closed-eye-isle", "name": "The Island of the Closed Eye", "land": "The seas", "kind": "sea", "size": 2, "x": 36.3, "y": 11.6, "lore": [["Marked and nothing else", "On one Registry chart this island carries a single closed eye and no name. No Pelagic Writ has ever granted a landing, and no ship's log mentions one. Fishermen from Echo Bay say it's a rock with birds on it, and they sail around it a long way."]]}, {"id": "saint-ordel", "name": "Last Bearing of the Saint Ordel", "land": "The seas", "kind": "sea", "size": 2, "x": 42, "y": 29, "lore": [["Unreturned", "The Saint Ordel was a Church-licensed ship, last logged on this bearing. It's still on the register of Unreturned ships, because nobody has been willing to make the loss official. Her owners' families still receive a stipend."]]}, {"id": "threnody", "name": "The Threnody Leviathan", "land": "The seas", "kind": "sea", "size": 1, "x": 83.8, "y": 47.4, "names": [["colonists", "the Chain"]], "lore": [["The bell platforms", "Iron bell platforms stand in the strait off the colony coast. One bell is rung for each soul the Church declares lost, and the colonists call the whole system the Chain. Ferries carrying the legally dead stop at each platform on their way out."], ["The Leviathan", "It drags ships on chains, and it moves when a loss is made official. Nobody describes it the same way twice, and the Church describes it as little as possible."]], "rumour": "The sea is hollow. Leviathan tunnels run under land and sea alike, and a few people can feel them move."}, {"id": "pale-expanse", "name": "The Pale Expanse", "land": "The seas", "kind": "sea", "size": 2, "x": 41, "y": 90, "lore": [["Milk water", "A stretch of Mare Scelus that the Registry only names. Here, at the bottom of the world near Gehenna's rim, the water goes milk-white and windless and the stars reflect in it too clearly. Ships drift for days, and crews report hearing their own conversations from the day before."]]}, {"id": "titan-at-sea", "name": "The Unfound Titan", "land": "The seas", "kind": "sea", "size": 2, "x": 56, "y": 77, "lore": [["The legend", "Smugglers in Scelis and Echo Bay tell of a stretch of water where the sea is flat on windy days, there are lights far below the hull, and charts get crossed out by the captains who drew them. Anyone who claims to have sailed it is lying or won't talk about it. The Church has no record of such a place."]]}, {"id": "terebra", "name": "Mount Terebra", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "holy", "size": 1, "x": 81, "y": 27, "names": [["Church Latin", "Mons Terebra, 'the Drill'"]], "lore": [["The tallest peak", "Terebra is the tallest mountain in Etelen, and it was believed to pierce Caelum. For centuries that made Pratum Putritum the holiest pilgrimage land there was. The name means the drill. The pilgrims took it as a compliment."], ["The climb", "The monks of Ur-Ignis climbed for decades and came within a few hundred feet of the peak. There they saw divine light leaking from a tear in the sky. The storm snuffed their incense, black decay bloomed on their skin, and they threw themselves from the cliffs as if they'd been told to."], ["Now", "No one climbs it. The pilgrims' stair still runs up from the Colonies, and the colonists use the lower flights as a lookout. Above the snowline the mountain is exactly as the monks left it."]]}, {"id": "scala-terebrae", "name": "Scala Terebrae", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "monument", "size": 2, "x": 80.8, "y": 32.6, "names": [["colonists", "the Scale"]], "lore": [["The pilgrim stair", "Nine thousand steps cut into the mountain's southern face, from the old pilgrim gate to the monastery. Every hundredth step has a shrine, and every shrine has a carved pilgrim kneeling in it. The Godrot reached the statues too. They have black veins in the stone, and they lean toward the drop in the same direction the monks fell."], ["The Scale", "The colonists call it the Scale, as in a debt you climb. The crime families use the lower flights as lookout posts over the Belt, and a man sent 'up the Scale' is being sent somewhere he won't come back from."]]}, {"id": "ur-ignis", "name": "Ruins of Ur-Ignis", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "wild", "size": 2, "x": 83.6, "y": 21.4, "lore": [["The first smoke", "The monastery on Terebra's slopes where the first Haustology was burned. After the monks' fall, the monastery caught Stiff-rot, which the world now calls Godrot. The incense experiments went on until there was nobody left to conduct them. One exiled monk's texts became the root of Smogmaw lore. His cell is the only one with a lock on the inside."]], "rumour": "Haustology's founding recipes were never fully recovered. Anyone with a strong stomach and a censer could still find them here."}, {"id": "vulnus-caeli", "name": "Vulnus Caeli", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 85.2, "y": 13.4, "names": [["colonists", "the Seam"]], "lore": [["The wound in heaven", "The monks saw divine light leaking through a tear in the sky just below the summit, and Church texts call it Vulnus Caeli, the Wound of Heaven. On clear nights the colonists below still see a pale seam over the peak that the stars don't cross. Saint Malchus blamed Godrot on 'reckless communion with the sacred flame' and wrote nothing about the seam."]]}, {"id": "malchus-hospice", "name": "Hospice of Saint Malchus", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 86.2, "y": 33.4, "lore": [["Where Vigour was written", "This was a pilgrim hospital on the eastern foothills where Saint Malchus wrote Vigour of the Lord while treating the first wave of Godrot. Now it stands roofless. Its beds are still in rows, with the iron frames rusted into the floor. Colonists come here to die privately, away from the flesh-traders, and leave their names scratched into the bed-frames so that someone remembers."]]}, {"id": "godrot-colonies", "name": "The Godrot Colonies", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "settlement", "size": 1, "x": 80, "y": 38.6, "names": [["Church Latin", "Coloniae Putridae"]], "lore": [["The slum", "Lawless exile settlements lie at Terebra's foot, for the rotting and for criminals considered too dangerous for the mercy of death. The Church supplies just enough for a slow death and seals the coast. Everything else is run by crime families who hold the water and the food."], ["Inside", "The Sluice Market, where the water comes in, belongs to whoever holds the sluice gates. Debts are kept in the Golden Room inside an aqueduct pillar. On the surface the Bent Reliquary is an inn where information is the only currency taken. There are also the Bone Chapel, the Tallow House and the Hollow Well, which everyone uses and nobody drinks from."], ["The flesh trade", "Healthy skin and limbs are stolen and grafted onto the rotting with a crude Godless Art. It's the Colonies' most profitable business and its most open secret. The gladiator pits feed it."], ["The legally dead", "A Canon of External Ledgers can declare a colonist legally dead, closing their account with the Church. The dead are ferried out past the bell platforms. It's the only way out, and everyone in the Colonies is trying to buy it."]], "rumour": "Whoever holds the sluice gates holds the Colonies. The current holder has a broken-chain sigil and a long memory."}, {"id": "ossarium-lazari", "name": "Ossarium Lazari", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 78, "y": 41, "names": [["colonists", "the Pits"]], "lore": [["Where the dead wait", "Lime pits outside the Colonies' seaward wall, where the dead are piled to wait for the Body Accounting boat. The flesh-traders get to them first. At night you can see lanterns moving down among the bodies, and the colonists' word for this is gleaning."]]}, {"id": "cingulum", "name": "The Cingulum", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "monument", "size": 2, "x": 75.6, "y": 33, "names": [["colonists", "the Belt"]], "lore": [["The sealed coast", "This is how the colony coast is sealed by Theurgists of every branch. A chain of squat towers runs along the entire shore, linked by iron booms across every inlet, and the name means girdle. Each tower keeps Fumatores' censers burning day and night against the rot. From the sea at night it looks like a coastline on fire."], ["The gate", "Supply barges dock at one gate and bring enough for a slow death. Nothing goes out except ash and ledgers."]], "rumour": "Rell's smugglers know a gap in the booms. So, it turns out, does someone in the Church."}, {"id": "statio-rationum", "name": "Statio Rationum", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "wild", "size": 2, "x": 68, "y": 31, "names": [["pious English", "the Body Accounting outpost"]], "lore": [["Where the dead are counted", "A Church outpost on the island west of the Colonies where the colony's dead are counted and their accounts closed. The clerks wear masks soaked in vinegar and handle the bodies with long hooks. The ledgers are copied in triplicate and shipped to the Cathedral. Nobody has ever asked why an outpost of counting-clerks needs a building twice the size of its offices."]]}, {"id": "requies", "name": "Requies", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "settlement", "size": 3, "x": 71.6, "y": 42.2, "lore": [["Outside the Belt", "A fishing hamlet on a rock just outside the Cingulum. The Church says it's named for eternal rest. The villagers know it's named for the rest the smugglers take there before the run in. Everyone in Requies is related to someone inside the Colonies, and everyone sells to Rell."]]}, {"id": "rubicun", "name": "Rubicun", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "holy", "size": 1, "x": 27, "y": 72.5, "names": [["farmers' Latin", "'the reddening'"]], "lore": [["On the head", "A marsh-town in the rock basin that was the colossus's faceless head. The houses stand on stilts above red-brown water, and the reeds grow in patterns that look deliberate from a height. Its people claim descent from the first landers who came ashore after the fall, and they won't let anyone forget it."], ["The rites", "Once a year Rubicun holds rites for the colossus's restless spirit. Phantom lights move over the marsh and voices whisper in a language no one admits to understanding. Spectral figures are seen often enough that nobody mentions them to strangers."], ["The cavern", "A cavern on the marsh's edge leads down into the head. Inside, past the last torch-niche, hangs a veil of black liquid that doesn't fall. Rubicun's elders forbid anyone to pass it. The few who have came back changed, knowing things, and wrong in the face."]]}, {"id": "vinea", "name": "Vinea", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 35.4, "y": 76.4, "lore": [["The vineyard", "Grape harvesters in the east work Sanguva vines that grow up out of the corpse. The grapes are dark enough to look black, with flesh the colour of liver. They regenerate blood fast and kill in excess."], ["The trade", "Officially the grapes are pressed into a tonic for Warsurgeons. Underground they go to Umorists and to the Merchants of Umor, who need a fast way to make back what their spells cost. Every family in Vinea has one relative who handles 'the other harvest'."]]}, {"id": "cor-profundum", "name": "Cor Profundum", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 29.8, "y": 78.2, "names": [["Lignum folk", "the Well"]], "lore": [["The heart-well", "A round sinkhole in the middle of Lignum, fifty yards across, where the soil gave way over the colossus's chest. Sanguva roots are thickest here and hang into the water like hair. The water rises and falls once a year, a full ten feet over one night, and every family in Lignum keeps a mark on their doorpost for the date. Nobody in Lignum calls it a heartbeat aloud."]], "rumour": "This year the water rose twice."}, {"id": "scelis", "name": "Scelis", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 20.8, "y": 80, "names": [["farmers' Latin", "'the crime'"]], "lore": [["Under the wings", "A lawless smugglers' refuge on the southwestern cliffs below the petrified wings. It's run by gangs, and every season brings rockfalls that kill someone. Its name comes from the first landers and is not a joke. If anyone sails without a writ, they crew up here, and no harbour in Etelen asks fewer questions."]], "rumour": "Old smugglers in Scelis remember captains who ran sealed boxes for 'the gentlemen', paid in Church silver, and changed their ship's name every season. Most of those captains are dead now, and not of age."}, {"id": "petrified-wings", "name": "The Petrified Wings", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "monument", "size": 1, "x": 17.8, "y": 70.4, "labelLeft": true, "lore": [["The cliffs", "The colossus fell from the sky under the 4th Archbeholder, and its wings turned to stone where they struck. Now they make up the western cliffs. The membrane has become slate, the ribs buttresses, and in low light the rock shows the same iridescent sheen as the scale under the soil."], ["What lives on them", "Seabirds nest in the folds by the hundred thousand and never land on the highest ridge. Scelis's smugglers climb the lower vanes for lookout. The joint where the wing meets the body is a gorge nobody has followed to the end."]]}, {"id": "costa", "name": "The Costa", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "monument", "size": 1, "x": 32.4, "y": 66.2, "names": [["pious English", "the Rib"]], "lore": [["A bridge of bone", "One of the colossus's ribs broke loose in the fall and landed across the strait to the northern island. It's half a mile of curved bone wide enough for two carts. Vinea's harvesters paved its back with cobbles and built toll-houses at each end, and they drive their grapes over it at harvest."], ["What the bone does", "It's warm in winter. After rain, marrow seeps up between the cobbles, and the cart-wheels go dark with it. The Sanguva vines at either end grow toward it, and every spring the toll-keepers cut them back from the road."]], "rumour": "Under the paving, sacred geometry is grown into the bone. People who carry anything touched by the colossi feel it pull the whole way across."}, {"id": "unguis", "name": "Unguis", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 26, "y": 87.2, "names": [["farmers' Latin", "'the claw'"]], "lore": [["The southern island", "An island off Lignum's south coast with a single hooked spur of scale-rock. The first landers thought it was a claw, and they were right. Nobody lives on it. Scelis smugglers use the lee of the hook to wait out patrols and say the wind makes a sound through it like someone breathing out."]]}, {"id": "lir-murrath", "name": "Lir-Murrath", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "monument", "size": 1, "x": 68, "y": 77, "names": [["old tongue", "'the place of the shut eye'"], ["Church Latin", "Statua Paenitentis"], ["pious English", "the Averted"]], "lore": [["The figure", "A kneeling figure carved from a single black hill, taller than the Cathedral's spires. Both hands are pressed over its eyes, and it faces away from the Eye, south toward Gehenna. The carving is too smooth for chisels and too regular for weather. The face behind the hands has never been seen."], ["Older than the Church", "It predates every Church record. The Scourging took whatever came before the 38th Age, and the Church's earliest surviving survey already calls it 'ancient'. The old-tongue name, Lir-Murrath, means the place of the shut eye. Murr, to shut, is the same root that's in Murrun, their plain word for night."], ["The Church's story", "The Church calls it Statua Paenitentis, the Penitent, and teaches that it shows the Blasphemer repenting under his brother's gaze. A chapel was built against its back three hundred years ago, and climbing the statue is forbidden on pain of excommunication."], ["The herders' story", "The herders say it shows someone who saw, and that the hands aren't hiding the eyes from the world but the world from the eyes. They graze their herds in its shadow and won't let them face the way it faces."]], "rumour": "Between two of its fingers there's a gap just wide enough to look through, and it was cut from the inside."}, {"id": "domus-bestiarum", "name": "Domus Bestiarum", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "holy", "size": 1, "x": 79.6, "y": 68.4, "names": [["herders", "Hesh-Kar, 'the beast-bearer'"]], "lore": [["The Frenatores' fortress", "A fortress-kennel on the northern headland where Dominus Bestia is taught. A copy of the Bestiarium Sanctus hangs chained in the chapter house, and novices read it standing up. Every Frenator in Etelen was bound here, and every one comes back to die here, as the order's rule requires."], ["The kennels", "A bound beast reflects its master's sins. A Gehennan summoner's shame comes out with teeth. So the kennel rows are a confession nobody chose to make, and the brothers are forbidden to walk another's row. The Caelum beasts are kept in a separate tower, and the brothers like them less."], ["The founder", "Enoch, the 57th Archbeholder, founded the Frenatores from a tome sealed beneath the Cathedral. He went mad, slaughtered many at the 345th Closing and was banished to Gehenna. The order doesn't hide this. It treats him as a warning carved over the gate."]], "rumour": "One cage has stood empty for three hundred years and is still fed every morning. It was Enoch's."}, {"id": "aunkar", "name": "Aunkar", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 69, "y": 68, "names": [["old tongue", "'sea-bearer'"], ["Church writs", "Portus Bestiae"]], "lore": [["The landing", "The only real port in Anima Bestia, facing the Eye across the strait. Church ships put in under the writ-name Portus Bestiae, and the town goes by its own name. Theurgists bound for the Domus come through in pairs with something hooded between them. The harbour dogs won't bark at a Frenator, and the town dislikes them for it."]]}, {"id": "ghelath", "name": "Ghelath", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "wild", "size": 2, "x": 75, "y": 80, "names": [["old tongue", "'the bone place'"]], "lore": [["Where herds go", "A low plain east of Lir-Murrath, white for miles with bone. The herds of Anima Bestia walk here to die, of their own accord, sometimes across half the continent. Herders follow them to the edge and no further. Frenatores hunt here for beasts that came back from somewhere else and walk in the wrong direction."]]}, {"id": "thol-hesh", "name": "Thol-Hesh", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 70.2, "y": 88.2, "names": [["old tongue", "'the burden of the herd'"]], "lore": [["The counting village", "The herders' largest village is in the southern woods. The morning after a Closing they count the herd twice, once for the beasts that are gone and once for the ones that weren't there before. The new ones are kept, named and never sold, and the person who counted them carries the burden of their names. That burden is the thol in the village's name, the same root as in Thol-Kar's title, Burden-Bearer."]]}, {"id": "kar-murrun", "name": "Kar-Murrun", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "settlement", "size": 3, "x": 87, "y": 79, "names": [["old tongue", "'bearer of the night'"]], "lore": [["The night market", "A hamlet that only trades after dark. Herders bring the animals they've decided to sell; outsiders bring salt, iron and Church silver. The rule is that nobody looks at anyone's face. It suits smugglers, Umorists and the occasional Frenator on business he'd rather the Domus didn't hear about."]]}, {"id": "limen-amonis", "name": "Limen Amonis", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 84, "y": 86.6, "names": [["pious English", "Amon's Threshold"], ["old tongue", "Saeltal, 'far below'"]], "lore": [["The border", "Legend says the spirit of Amon, the First Demonward, guards the border between Lutum and Gehenna. Here the land stops above a red haze at the bottom rim of the world. Ash drifts up, not down."], ["The pilgrimage", "Every Venator comes here once in their life and leaves an eye from their first kill in the niches of the rock face. Some of the niches are very old, and a few hold eyes that are still wet."]]}, {"id": "lir-aun", "name": "Lir-Aun", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 92.4, "y": 55.5, "names": [["old tongue", "'the sea's eye'"]], "lore": [["The islets", "A scatter of islets off the east coast. The herders say the sea opens an eye here. Every few years a round patch of water goes perfectly still and dark among them, while the sea around it keeps moving. Nothing grows on the side of the rocks that faces it."]]}, {"id": "domus-matthiae", "name": "Domus Matthiae", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 11, "y": 19, "names": [["pious English", "the Pilgrim's Return"]], "lore": [["The returned man", "Pilgrim Matthias walked into Gehenna and out again and wrote Journey of Man Through Hell. This monastery stands on the hillside where he was found, barefoot, burned to the knees and still writing. The monks keep his original manuscript in a cell with no door, and they read it through a grille by candlelight, one page a day."], ["The copyists", "The monastery's trade is copying the Journey for every parish in Etelen. Each copyist is allowed to copy only one chapter in their lifetime, so no single brother has written out the whole book. The abbot says this is humility. The brothers say it's because the copyists who did the whole thing stopped sleeping."]], "rumour": "A copy of the Journey surfaces with a chapter no other copy has, describing a part of Gehenna shaped like a vast eye that is closed."}, {"id": "campanaria", "name": "Campanaria", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "settlement", "size": 2, "x": 24.2, "y": 30.8, "names": [["pious English", "Belltown"]], "lore": [["The bell foundry", "Church bells keep demons back, and nearly every bell in Iuga Domini was cast here. The town is built around three great casting pits, and on pouring days the whole valley smells of hot bronze and burnt hair. A bell isn't finished until it has been rung through one Closing in the foundry tower. Bells that crack in that test are melted down. Bells that ring true are sold with a certificate."], ["The tongue-makers", "The clappers are made by a separate guild that keeps its alloy secret. People in town say a little of the founder's own blood goes into every clapper, and that this is why a Campanaria bell can be heard by things that have no ears."]], "rumour": "A parish bell from Campanaria rang on its own on the last Closing, three villages away from any church."}, {"id": "vigils-cross", "name": "Vigil's Cross", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 18.4, "y": 36.6, "lore": [["The crossroads", "Where the Tithe Road meets the Wick Road there's a stone shrine with an oculus carved through its top, so that the Eye can look down through it, and a gibbet beside it. The Church hangs Apostates here, in secret and at night, and leaves them up until the crows are done. Travellers leave a coin in the shrine for the Eye and a stone under the gibbet for whoever's on it, and they never explain the stone."]]}, {"id": "porta-spinae", "name": "Porta Spinae", "land": "Iuga Domini", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 24.8, "y": 23.6, "names": [["pious English", "the Spine Gate"]], "lore": [["The blockade fort", "A squat Oculate fort across the mouth of the canyon pass through the Ossuar Spine. When the pass is closed, a chain as thick as a man's leg is drawn across the road, and every traveller is turned back to Dunridge whatever papers they carry. The garrison rotates every thirty days. The soldiers say that's because nobody can stand the sound the canyon makes at night for longer."]]}, {"id": "rupes-iabalis", "name": "Rupes Iabalis", "land": "The seas", "kind": "holy", "size": 3, "x": 33.6, "y": 20.4, "names": [["pious English", "Jabal's Rock"]], "lore": [["Where Jabal was born mute", "A bare island off Iuga Domini's coast where Jabal, founder of the Battle Choirs, was born without a voice. The only building is a roofless chapel where the Arias come once in their lives to sing the Rite of Light to an empty sea. On still days fishermen hear the Rite coming off the rock when nobody is there."]]}, {"id": "aquae-lazari", "name": "Aquae Lazari", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "monument", "size": 2, "x": 83.4, "y": 37.4, "names": [["colonists", "the Long Drip"]], "lore": [["The aqueduct", "This aqueduct was built for pilgrims, carrying snowmelt from Terebra down to the old hospice towns on arches a hundred feet high. Now it feeds the Colonies. It leaks along its whole length, and the leaks are owned: every drip has a family under it with buckets and a knife. The Golden Room, where the debts of the Colonies are kept, is hollowed out inside one of its pillars."], ["Who holds the water", "Whoever holds the sluice gates where the aqueduct enters the Colonies holds the Colonies. Wars here are fought over stone channels, and the losers are found in the water."]]}, {"id": "spelunca-silae", "name": "Spelunca Silae", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 87.6, "y": 24.5, "names": [["pious English", "Silas's Cave"]], "lore": [["The Smogmaw's den", "Silas the Smogmaw lived here after the Venatores cast him out. He was the last to take beast-form from the forbidden smoke and keep his mind. The walls are black to the height of a man with old smoke, scored with claw marks above that, and below the marks someone has written a recipe in a careful clerical hand. Every Smogmaw since has made the pilgrimage, and the recipe has been half scratched out by the ones who didn't come back."]]}, {"id": "primus-portus", "name": "Primus Portus", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "settlement", "size": 3, "x": 36.8, "y": 71.5, "names": [["Lignum folk", "the Landing"]], "lore": [["Where the first landers came ashore", "A shingle beach on the east coast where the first boats grounded after the colossus fell. There's a fishing hamlet now and a stone marking the spot. The shingle is not stone. It's shed scale, worn round by the tide, and it chimes underfoot. Rubicun and Vinea both claim the first landers as ancestors, and they hold rival ceremonies on this beach on the same day."]]}, {"id": "squamae", "name": "Squamae", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "wild", "size": 3, "x": 24.5, "y": 64.4, "names": [["Lignum folk", "the Shed Fields"]], "lore": [["The scale fields", "Along the north coast the soil is so thin that the colossus's skin shows through in plates the size of barn doors. They're iridescent, faintly warm, and always clean, even after a storm. Scale-cutters pry loose the edges for armour and roof tiles. It's legal and lucrative, and the cutters never take more than one plate from the same place, though none of them can say why."]]}, {"id": "medulla", "name": "Medulla", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "kind": "settlement", "size": 3, "x": 37, "y": 61.5, "names": [["Lignum folk", "Marrowtown"]], "lore": [["The far end of the Rib", "A hamlet on the northern island at the far end of the Costa, where the marrow that seeps through the bridge's paving is collected in gutters and boiled down. Marrow-tallow burns without smoke and doesn't go rancid, and the Alkemi Ordained buy every pot. The people of Medulla are pale and long-lived and have no children, and they don't find either thing strange."]]}, {"id": "vaelunath", "name": "Vaelunath", "land": "Anima Bestia", "kind": "holy", "size": 2, "x": 64.6, "y": 74, "names": [["old tongue", "'the place of the unseen hour'"]], "lore": [["The Closing ring", "A ring of standing stones on the western hills where the herders bring their animals on Closing night. They don't pray. They stand inside the ring with their herds all night, counting out loud in the old tongue, and anything that comes out of the dark and joins the count is let in. In the morning the stones are wet, even in drought."], ["What the Church says", "Church surveys call it a pagan fold and report that the herds kept here turn less often than herds anywhere else in Etelen. No Church survey has explained why, and the Frenatores have stopped asking to study it."]], "rumour": "Last Closing the count came out one higher than the herd, and nobody can find the extra animal."}], "routes": [{"id": "via-lucis", "name": "Via Lucis", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "road", "size": 1, "points": [[17, 17], [19.5, 16.3], [22.5, 15.6], [25.5, 14.6], [27.6, 14]], "names": [["pious English", "the Flame Road"]], "lore": [["City to sea", "The paved road from the City of Lanterns down to Echo Bay, the oldest in Iuga Domini. Once a year after the Ceremony of Eternal Light, a Keeper carries a flame from the Lantern down this road in a sealed lamp, on foot, to relight the lantern of the Custos Velatus. Traffic stops for the procession and travellers kneel at the roadside. Shrines every mile hold a lamp lit from the procession, so that on a clear night the road can be seen from the sea as a line of lights coming down from the mountains."]], "purpose": "pilgrim"}, {"id": "via-peregrina", "name": "Via Peregrina", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "road", "size": 1, "points": [[17, 17], [19.6, 20], [22.5, 21.6], [24.8, 23.6], [26.2, 26], [27.4, 28.6], [28.4, 31], [29.6, 35], [31, 38.5]], "names": [["pious English", "the Pilgrim Road"]], "lore": [["The way to the Eye", "The pilgrims' road runs from the City across the Ossuar Spine by the canyon pass, through Dunridge, down to the coast at Ostium Gratiae and on to the first arch of the Pons Peregrinorum. Every pilgrim to the Cathedral walks at least part of it. It's lined with hostels, relic-sellers and graves, the last of those mostly for people who didn't get far."], ["When the pass is shut", "When the Church closes Porta Spinae the road stops dead at Dunridge, and pilgrims pile up there until it opens again. There is a way under the Spine, but pilgrims are told it's for the damned."]], "purpose": "pilgrim"}, {"id": "pons-span", "name": "The span of the Pons", "land": "The seas", "route": "bridge", "size": 1, "points": [[31, 38.5], [33.6, 39.8], [36, 41.2], [38.2, 42.6], [40.6, 44.2]], "lore": [["Forty miles of arches", "The Pons Peregrinorum itself, running from the shore out over the shoals toward the Eye. It ends at the last half-arch. See the Pons Peregrinorum entry for its history."]], "purpose": "pilgrim"}, {"id": "tithe-road", "name": "The Tithe Road", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "road", "size": 1, "points": [[17, 17], [16.2, 22], [15.6, 28.4], [16.6, 33.4], [18.4, 36.6], [20.4, 39.4], [22, 42], [23.8, 45], [25, 47.4]], "names": [["Church Latin", "Via Decimae"]], "lore": [["The grain road", "The main road south from the City through the farming country to the edge of the Sylvan Veil. Every autumn the tithe-carts come up it loaded with grain, wool and wax for the City, and every spring they go back down loaded with blessings, bells and priests. It's the road most people in Iuga Domini mean when they say 'the road', and the Church keeps a shelter on it for anyone caught out on a Closing night."]], "purpose": "trade"}, {"id": "bell-road", "name": "The Bell Road", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "road", "size": 2, "points": [[18.4, 36.6], [20.6, 34], [22.4, 32.6], [24.2, 30.8], [26.2, 26]], "lore": [["Bronze on wheels", "A short road that links the Tithe Road to Dunridge through Titheford and Campanaria. New bells travel it on low wagons pulled by twelve oxen, with a priest walking in front ringing a handbell so that nothing on the road mistakes the big bell's silence for an invitation."]], "purpose": "trade"}, {"id": "wick-road", "name": "The Wick Road", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "road", "size": 2, "points": [[9, 22], [9.6, 26.2], [10.8, 30.2], [12.4, 34.4], [14, 38], [16.2, 37.4], [18.4, 36.6]], "lore": [["Tallow and ore", "A rough western road that carries haskite down from Turibulum and tallow out of Cerafax, joining the Tithe Road at Vigil's Cross. It passes through Kartoffel and a dozen villages like it. The carters on this road are famous for never stopping at night, whatever they hear on the verge."]], "purpose": "trade"}, {"id": "cove-path", "name": "The Cove Path", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "track", "size": 2, "points": [[14, 38], [12.8, 40.2], [11.6, 42], [10.4, 43.8], [9.4, 45.4]], "lore": [["Down to the sea", "A cart track from Kartoffel's fields west toward the coast, which fades into a sheep path and then into nothing above Cala Signi. Kartoffel's priest has forbidden the village to use it. The ruts suggest someone does."]], "purpose": "smuggling"}, {"id": "belt-road", "name": "The Belt Road", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "route": "road", "size": 2, "points": [[74.6, 22], [75.2, 27.6], [75.6, 33], [76.4, 38], [77.6, 42], [80.6, 44], [84.4, 44.6]], "lore": [["The patrol road", "The road behind the towers of the Cingulum, walked day and night by Theurgist patrols in vinegar masks. It is the only road in Pratum Putritum kept in repair. Colonists caught on it are shot from the towers without challenge, so its verges are the safest place in the land to hide something."]], "purpose": "church"}, {"id": "scale-road", "name": "The Scale", "land": "Pratum Putritum", "route": "road", "size": 2, "points": [[80, 38.6], [80.8, 32.6], [81.2, 29.4], [81, 27], [82.4, 24], [83.6, 21.4]], "lore": [["Up the mountain", "The Scala Terebrae drawn as a road: from the Colonies up nine thousand steps to the ruins of Ur-Ignis. Only its lower flights are walked now."]], "purpose": "pilgrim"}, {"id": "harvest-road", "name": "The Harvest Road", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "route": "road", "size": 1, "points": [[35.4, 76.4], [36.8, 71.5], [35.2, 68.6], [32.4, 66.2], [34.6, 63.6], [37, 61.5]], "lore": [["Grapes to the sea", "Vinea's road north to the Landing and across the Costa to Medulla, where the grape barges load. At harvest it's one continuous line of carts, and the vintners hire Scelis toughs to ride the barrels, because Sanguva grapes are worth killing for."]], "purpose": "trade"}, {"id": "reed-causeway", "name": "The Reed Causeway", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "route": "road", "size": 2, "points": [[35.4, 76.4], [32.6, 77.6], [29.8, 78.2], [28.2, 75.4], [27, 72.5]], "lore": [["Across the marsh", "A road of bundled reeds and scale slabs laid across the marsh from Vinea to Rubicun, passing the rim of Cor Profundum. It sinks a little every year and is relaid every spring. Travellers keep to the middle, because the marsh on either side of the causeway is deeper than it looks and sometimes warm."]], "purpose": "trade"}, {"id": "smugglers-climb", "name": "The Smugglers' Climb", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "route": "track", "size": 2, "points": [[27, 72.5], [24.4, 74.4], [22.4, 77], [20.8, 80]], "lore": [["Under the wings", "A goat-path from Rubicun down the cliffs to Scelis. It's quicker than any road and gets someone killed by rockfall most years. Smugglers use it in the dark, roped together."]], "purpose": "smuggling"}, {"id": "long-drove", "name": "The Long Drove", "land": "Anima Bestia", "route": "road", "size": 1, "points": [[69, 68], [67.6, 71.4], [68, 77], [71.6, 78.6], [75, 80]], "names": [["old tongue", "Hesh-Saelun, 'the far hour of the herd'"]], "lore": [["The herders' road", "Anima Bestia's one long road is a drove-way from the port at Aunkar, past Lir-Murrath, to the bone plain at Ghelath, trodden by herds rather than built. It's a hundred yards wide in places and churned to mud. The herds walk it in one direction only. Herders say a beast that walks it the other way is not the same beast that went down it."]], "purpose": "drove"}, {"id": "south-drove", "name": "The Southern Droves", "land": "Anima Bestia", "route": "track", "size": 2, "points": [[64.6, 74], [66.4, 75.6], [68, 77], [69.4, 82.4], [70.2, 88.2], [75.6, 86], [81, 82], [87, 79], [84, 86.6]], "lore": [["Between the villages", "Drove-paths linking Vaelunath, Lir-Murrath, Thol-Hesh and the night market at Kar-Murrun, with a branch to Limen Amonis that only Venatores take. There are no inns. Herders take in travellers who know to count the herd with them before sleeping."]], "purpose": "drove"}, {"id": "writ-lane", "name": "The Writ Lane", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "size": 1, "points": [[29, 15], [34, 24], [39.5, 32], [44.6, 40.6]], "lore": [["The licensed way", "The main sea lane from Echo Bay to the Cathedral. Every ship on it carries a writ and a Witness navigator, and Church cutters check both. It keeps well south of the Black Meridian."]], "purpose": "trade"}, {"id": "colony-run", "name": "The Colony Run", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "size": 1, "points": [[29.4, 32], [36, 30], [45, 33.8], [56.4, 40.4], [64, 41.6], [71, 40.6], [76.6, 40.2]], "lore": [["Supply and silence", "The lane from Ostium Gratiae to the Cingulum's gate. Supply barges take it out full and bring back ledgers. On the way back crews burn juniper on deck the whole voyage and don't talk to each other until they've cleared quarantine."]], "purpose": "church"}, {"id": "penitents-crossing", "name": "The Penitents' Crossing", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "size": 2, "points": [[50, 61.6], [56, 65], [62, 66.8], [69, 68]], "lore": [["To the beasts", "The short lane from Portus Oculi to Aunkar, carrying Frenatores, their novices and their cages. Sailors don't like the crossing because the cargo is noisy at night, and sometimes it's noisy in voices."]], "purpose": "church"}, {"id": "vine-lane", "name": "The Vine Lane", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "size": 2, "points": [[37, 61.5], [42, 60.4], [46.4, 58.4]], "lore": [["Grapes to the Cathedral", "Barges from Medulla carry Sanguva wine and marrow-tallow to the Cathedral's harbour. It's officially for the Warsurgeons. The barges sit low in the water, lower than wine accounts for."]], "purpose": "trade"}, {"id": "the-chain-lane", "name": "The Chain", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "size": 2, "points": [[77.6, 42.6], [80.4, 45.6], [83.8, 47.4], [88, 48.6]], "lore": [["Past the bells", "The route the ferries of the legally dead take, platform to platform past the bells. Every bell rings once as a ferry passes it, and the passengers count them."]], "purpose": "church"}, {"id": "iter-terebrae", "name": "Iter Terebrae", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "purpose": "pilgrim", "size": 1, "points": [[29, 13], [37, 9.4], [46, 8.6], [56, 11], [64, 15], [70, 19], [74.4, 22.4]], "names": [["pious English", "the Pilgrims' Crossing"]], "lore": [["The old crossing", "Before the Godrot, pilgrims bound for Mount Terebra sailed from Echo Bay along the top of the world, keeping the Eye on their right hand the whole way so it could watch them go. It was the busiest sea lane in Etelen for three centuries. Now it is forbidden. The landing at the far end lies inside the Cingulum, and the Church struck the route from every chart printed after the coast was sealed."], ["What's left", "Fishermen still find pilgrim tokens in their nets along this line: small lead eyes stamped with the date of a crossing. Most of the dates are from the year the rot began."]], "rumour": "A ship was seen on the old lane last spring, flying pilgrim colours, with nobody on deck."}, {"id": "saints-circuit", "name": "The Saints' Circuit", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "track", "purpose": "pilgrim", "size": 2, "points": [[17, 17], [21, 12.6], [17, 13], [13, 13.5], [11, 19], [14.4, 18.4], [17, 17]], "lore": [["The mountain houses", "A loop of mountain paths from the City to the three great houses above it: the Domus Eliadae, Arx Amonis and Domus Matthiae, then back. Pious townsfolk walk it once a year in a single day, barefoot, which takes from before dawn to after dark. Each house gives walkers a token, a stitch, an eye and a page, and someone holding all three is said to be ready for anything Gehenna could show them."]], "rumour": "Walkers who finish after dark sometimes count four houses on the circuit, not three."}, {"id": "singers-crossing", "name": "The Singers' Crossing", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "purpose": "pilgrim", "size": 2, "points": [[28.6, 15.2], [31, 17.6], [33.6, 20.4]], "lore": [["Out to Jabal's Rock", "The short crossing from Echo Bay to Rupes Iabalis, made by every Aria once in their lives. The boatmen are paid double to keep silent, because the choir sings the whole way out and the boatmen are not supposed to join in."]]}, {"id": "rite-way", "name": "The Rite Way", "land": "Lignum Stagnae", "route": "track", "purpose": "pilgrim", "size": 2, "points": [[36.8, 71.5], [33.4, 70.4], [30, 71], [27, 72.5]], "lore": [["Landing to the head", "The procession route of the first landers, from the shingle at Primus Portus up onto the colossus's head at Rubicun. On the night of the yearly rite, Rubicun and Vinea walk it in two separate processions, an hour apart, each carrying lamps and each pretending the other isn't there. The path is worn a foot deep into the scale."]]}, {"id": "last-walk", "name": "The Venators' Last Walk", "land": "Anima Bestia", "route": "track", "purpose": "pilgrim", "size": 2, "points": [[69, 68], [67.6, 71.4], [69.4, 79], [75, 84.2], [80, 85.8], [84, 86.6]], "lore": [["To the edge of the world", "The path every Venator walks once, from the landing at Aunkar to Limen Amonis. They go alone, without a lamp, and they don't hunt on the way, whatever they meet. The herders leave bread on their doorsteps for passing Venators and don't come out to watch them go by."]]}, {"id": "salt-road", "name": "The Salt Road", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "road", "purpose": "trade", "size": 2, "points": [[27.6, 14], [28.4, 19], [28.8, 24], [27.8, 28], [28.4, 31]], "lore": [["Fish and salt", "The coast road from Echo Bay down to Ostium Gratiae, carrying salt fish south and salt north. It's windy, slow and safe, with a fish-curing village in every cove. Carters say you can follow it blind by the smell."]]}, {"id": "haskite-lane", "name": "The Haskite Lane", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "road", "purpose": "trade", "size": 2, "points": [[9, 22], [11.6, 20.6], [14.6, 18.4], [17, 17]], "lore": [["Ore to the forges", "A steep paved lane that brings Turibulum's tarnished haskite up to the Alkemi forges in the City. Oculates escort every wagon, because the ore is worth more than its weight in silver once it's been worked. Most of it never comes back down the lane as anything but finished censers."]]}, {"id": "red-road", "name": "The Red Road", "land": "Iuga Domini", "route": "track", "purpose": "trade", "size": 3, "points": [[28.4, 31], [24.2, 30.8], [20.6, 34], [17.6, 40.2], [14, 38], [10.8, 30.2], [6.6, 33]], "lore": [["The Merchants of Umor", "No map marks it. The Red Road is the circuit the Merchants of Umor ride from village to village, selling vials of their own spell-blood out of the backs of painted wagons. Their left arms are scarred from the wrist to the elbow. Villages along the route leave a red rag tied to a fence post when they want the caravan to stop."]], "rumour": "This season a new caravan is selling written spells, not blood. The other merchants won't park next to it."}, {"id": "scale-run", "name": "The Scale Run", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "purpose": "trade", "size": 2, "points": [[37.6, 71.4], [42.6, 67.4], [48.6, 63.8]], "lore": [["Armour off the corpse", "Barges carry cut scale from Lignum's Shed Fields to the Cathedral's armourers. Scale plate turns a sword better than steel and weighs half as much, and only Theurgists are allowed to wear it."]]}, {"id": "black-water-run", "name": "The Black Water Run", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "purpose": "smuggling", "size": 2, "points": [[20, 81.6], [15.6, 75.6], [14.6, 66], [13.6, 58], [12.4, 51.6], [9.6, 46.4]], "lore": [["Scelis to the sign", "The unlicensed run from the smugglers' docks at Scelis, up the strait west of Lignum, to Cala Signi on Iuga Domini's coast. It's sailed at night without lights and steered by the cliffs. Crews who run it don't carry writs, names or anything with their own handwriting on it."]], "rumour": "Old hands say there used to be a third leg to the run, out past the sign into open water, and that nobody has sailed it in twenty years."}, {"id": "the-gap", "name": "The Gap", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "purpose": "smuggling", "size": 3, "points": [[71.6, 42.2], [73.6, 40], [75.4, 37.4]], "lore": [["Through the Belt", "The way small boats from Requies slip through the Cingulum's booms on moonless nights, carrying medicine, news and fresh skin in, and people out. Its exact line changes every season, and only one family in Requies knows the current one."]]}, {"id": "night-trail", "name": "The Night Trail", "land": "Anima Bestia", "route": "track", "purpose": "smuggling", "size": 3, "points": [[92.6, 72.6], [90, 75.6], [87, 79]], "lore": [["From the east beach", "A trail from a hidden beach on Anima Bestia's east coast up to the night market at Kar-Murrun. Boats land here that don't want to be logged at Aunkar, carrying Umorists, runaway novices from the Domus and goods the Church would confiscate."]]}, {"id": "ledger-lane", "name": "The Ledger Lane", "land": "The seas", "route": "sea", "purpose": "church", "size": 2, "points": [[67, 34], [61, 40], [55.6, 46]], "lore": [["The dead, in triplicate", "The Church's fast sloops carry the death-ledgers of the Colonies from the Statio Rationum to the Cathedral's archives. They never stop and never take passengers. Fishermen give them the right of way, and some make the sign of the Eye as they pass."]]}]};
